com/sb/yfCPb-bOVKM/storyboard3_L$L/$N.jpg?sqp=-oaymwGhAUg48quKqQOYAYgBAZUBAAAEQpgBMqABPKgBBLIBQBANDBAVHyYtDg4PEhcrLCkPDhAVHyoyKQ8RFBgmPTgtERQeKjFLRzYVHCkuOUdNPyUuNz1HUlFFM0BCQ0xERkO6AUARERUjRENDQxETFi9DQ0NDFRYpQ0NDQ0MjL0NDQ0NDQ0RDQ0NDQ0JCQ0NDQ0NCQkJDQ0NDQkJCQkNDQ0JCQkJCovOX_wMGCKLQssUG|48#27#100#10#10#0#default#rs$AOn4CLAC3R0EZq5KVb0KXznav2O84UUoPQ|80#45#208#10#10#10000#M$M#rs$AOn4CLDllpqTJkbEtkKEw2BRuCH5Xcebxg|160#90#208#5#5#10000#M$M#rs$AOn4CLAu54e8t9INs4Cq2orCL0RWB56S3w|320#180#208#3#3#10000#M$M#rs$AOn4CLArF8v4Fmkz6U3Spj6rUv0-73k6KQ","recommendedLevel":3,"fineScrubbingRecommendedLevel":2,"highResolutionRecommendedLevel":3}},"microformat":{"playerMicroformatRenderer":{"thumbnail":{"thumbnails":[{"url":"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/yfCPb-bOVKM/maxresdefault.jpg?sqp=-oaymwEmCIAKENAF8quKqQMa8AEB-AH-CYAC0AWKAgwIABABGGcgZyhnMA8=\u0026rs=AOn4CLBk2hTTgTk4T8ipS77L_upuCyaPCg","width":1280,"height":720}]},"embed":{"iframeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/yfCPb-bOVKM","width":1280,"height":720},"title":{"simpleText":"S1E9: Gene's Terrifying MySQL Migration: Google AI Studio to the Rescue and the Hot Hand Fallacy"},"description":{"simpleText":"You've probably already experienced this: vibe coding dramatically accelerates development work, but this velocity comes with a hidden cost: when you're accomplishing far more in shorter timeframes, you're statistically bound to have more accidents - and potentially more catastrophic ones. This reality becomes even more dangerous when combined with what psychologists call the \"hot hand fallacy,\" where recent successes create an illusion of predictable continued success.\n\nIn this cautionary tale, Gene shares his harrowing experience migrating from MySQL 5 to MySQL 8 - a task he'd avoided for years until Google Cloud's cost warning finally motivated him to act. What started as a straightforward database upgrade became a disaster when he attempted to migrate his Google Looker Studio dashboards. After successfully handling the database migration and authentication fixes, Gene made a critical error: he began modifying the dashboard connectors without creating backups. Within hours, his functional analytics dashboard had transformed into what he described as \"a pile of rubble\" - displaying cryptic error messages with no version control to restore it.\n\nFinding himself in what he compared to piloting an aircraft when all displays go dark, Gene turned to an unexpected rescue tool: Gemini's screen-sharing capability. For nearly two hours past midnight, he used the AI as an intelligent debugging companion, sharing screenshots of his broken dashboard and receiving guided explanations of Looker Studio's architecture. While Gemini wasn't a Looker expert and couldn't provide exact navigation instructions, it served as what Gene called \"an amazing rubber duck\" - one that talked back, recognized the systems involved, and provided reassuring guidance through systematic troubleshooting.\n\nSteve and Gene discuss the \"hot hand fallacy,\" a dangerous psychological trap where successive AI coding wins create an illusion of building trust with the technology, when users are actually just developing better prompting skills. Both hosts confess to similar overconfident mistakes within the same week, leading to their key insight: AI tools remain fundamentally unpredictable regardless of past performance, capable of making \"the stupidest of all possible options\" at any moment. They acknowledge that when you're coding faster and being more ambitious with AI assistance, you'll inevitably have more train wrecks - but that's part of the territory. \n\nTest pilot Frank Borman (of Apollo 8 fame) said, \"Use your superior judgment to avoid situations where you have to rely on your superior skill.\" A golden rule for AI-assisted development: everything must have reliable backups and rollback mechanisms, because no amount of previous success guarantees future reliability. The episode demonstrates both recovery techniques (switching from high-speed \"vibe coding\" to deliberate \"system two\" thinking) and prevention strategies (proper planning, staging environments, and backup systems) for when AI-powered ambition meets production reality.\n\nPre-order the book here: https://www.amazon.com/Vibe-Coding-Building-Production-Grade-Software/dp/1966280025\n\n00:00 Introduction to the Vibe Coding Podcast\n00:31 Exploring AFRI and Dungeons \u0026 Dragons\n01:30 Show and Tell: Markdown Parser and Impossible Tasks\n01:55 The MySQL Migration Adventure\n02:54 Overcoming Database Challenges\n06:56 The Looker Studio Migration Disaster\n12:58 Gemini bad audio\n13:30 more here\n16:42 Lessons Learned and Best Practices\n29:25 The Dual Modes of Vibe Coding\n29:50 The Exhausting Reality of Context Switching\n30:25 The Relief of Detailed Pair Programming\n31:09 The MySQL Migration Success\n32:00 begin\n32:06 The Illusion of Trust in AI\n34:22 DEND\n34:22 The Chow Tool Development Journey\n34:22 The Future of the Chow Tool\n34:22 Concluding Thoughts and Future Plans"},"lengthSeconds":"2063","ownerProfileUrl":"http://www.youtube.com/@VibeCodingWithSteveandGene","externalChannelId":"UCXMJymhxQMMB_iLzt5VMbsg","isFamilySafe":true,"availableCountries":["AD","AE","AF","AG","AI","AL","AM","AO","AQ","AR","AS","AT","AU","AW","AX","AZ","BA","BB","BD","BE","BF","BG","BH","BI","BJ","BL","BM","BN","BO","BQ","BR","BS","BT","BV","BW","BY","BZ","CA","CC","CD","CF","CG","CH","CI","CK","CL","CM","CN","CO","CR","CU","CV","CW","CX","CY","CZ","DE","DJ","DK","DM","DO","DZ","EC","EE","EG","EH","ER","ES","ET","FI","FJ","FK","FM","FO","FR","GA","GB","GD","GE","GF","GG","GH","GI","GL","GM","GN","GP","GQ","GR","GS","GT","GU","GW","GY","HK","HM","HN","HR","HT","HU","ID","IE","IL","IM","IN","IO","IQ","IR","IS","IT","JE","JM","JO","JP","KE","KG","KH","KI","KM","KN","KP","KR","KW","KY","KZ","LA","LB","LC","LI","LK","LR","LS","LT","LU","LV","LY","MA","MC","MD","ME","MF","MG","MH","MK","ML","MM","MN","MO","MP","MQ","MR","MS","MT","MU","MV